# 🌿📜 Living Vedanta Library

## योगवासिष्ठ ब्रह्मगीता

### **सर्ग १८८**

## **आतिवाहिक देह और जीव का रहस्य**

### *The Mystery of the Subtle Body and the Reflected Self*

**सादर प्रणाम।**

Having revealed that **all this is Brahman alone**, Maharṣi Vasiṣṭha now turns our attention to a profound question:

**If Brahman alone exists, who is this individual "I" that appears to wander through birth, death, pleasure, sorrow, bondage and liberation?**

The answer is astonishing.

The sages explain that what we ordinarily call the *jīva* is not an independently existing entity at all. It is merely the **reflection of Pure Consciousness (चिदाभास)** appearing through the subtle body, known as the **Ātivāhika Deha**.

Like a city seen in a dream...
Like a castle imagined in thought...
Like reflections dancing upon water...

the individual soul appears to journey through countless experiences, yet never possesses an independent reality.

The universe itself is described in exactly the same way.

It appears.

It functions.

It seems to contain action, enjoyment, suffering, striving and liberation.

Yet, upon true inquiry, it is discovered to be nothing more than a projection within Consciousness itself.

Maharṣi Vasiṣṭha therefore declares:

> **When this immeasurable appearance of the universe seems to arise, in truth nothing whatsoever has arisen.**

Why?

Because the limitless Self alone pervades everything.

Nothing has ever departed from It.

Nothing has ever been added to It.

Nothing has ever truly happened to It.

The purpose of this Sarga is not to deny our experience, but to reveal its true ontological status.

The dream may appear vivid.

The dreamer alone is real.

Likewise, the universe may appear vast and endlessly diversified, yet the substratum is ever the one indivisible Consciousness.

As we begin this new chapter, let us contemplate not merely **what appears**, but **that in which all appearances arise, shine, and dissolve**.

May Maharṣi Vasiṣṭha guide our inquiry.

May Bhagavān Śrī Rāma inspire our earnest listening.

May this satsang become an opportunity not merely to understand Vedānta intellectually, but to awaken to the silent Reality that has never ceased to be our own Self.

**हरिः ॐ तत्सत्**॥
